-- `Order` is a value with an order of its own
-- (`docs/design/static-dispatch-spike.md` §3.2, §9.4, A.63).
--
-- `Order`'s row in §3.2's table is `derived compare` and not a primitive,
-- and the reason is one line of JavaScript: its constructors are bare tag
-- strings (`backend.md` §4), so `<` on them is ALPHABETIC —
-- `"EQ" < "GT" < "LT"` — where the language means `LT < EQ < GT`. The
-- `$order` table of §9.4 is what makes the declaration order the answer,
-- and these lines are what a wrong one prints: `LT < GT` would be `False`
-- and `EQ < GT` would be `True` either way, so the first two lines alone
-- would not catch it.
--
-- `core/Basics.beni` declares a `pub compare` of its own, which is exactly
-- why the table is consulted BEFORE the module rule (§3.2's opening
-- paragraph, A.63): asking the module rule first left `Basics.Order` with
-- no derived row at all while every other module's `<` on an `Order` named
-- `ext_derived Basics.Order compare` — a call to a function that was never
-- written.
--
-- The record is the second half: an `Order` reached through a derived
-- shape, so the evidence slot of §8.2 holds `Basics$Order$$compare` by
-- name rather than the comparison being inlined.
import Node exposing (Program)


type alias Step =
    { name : String, verdict : Order }


show : Bool -> String
show b =
    if b then
        "True"

    else
        "False"


main : Program
main =
    Node.printLines
        [ show (LT < GT)
        , show (GT < LT)
        , show (LT < EQ)
        , show (EQ < GT)
        , show (GT <= GT)
        , show (LT == LT)
        , show (LT /= GT)
        , show ({ name = "a", verdict = LT } < { name = "a", verdict = GT })
        , show ({ name = "a", verdict = GT } < { name = "a", verdict = LT })
        , show ({ name = "a", verdict = EQ } == { name = "a", verdict = EQ })
        ]
